    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d199">

                <pb facs="0573" n="543" sameAs="frus:frus1949v01/pg543"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Department of State Atomic Energy Files</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chairman of the Joint Congressional Committee on
                        Atomic Energy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">McMahon</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to President Truman </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d199-f1"
                    />
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-09-28">September 28,
                        1949</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. President</hi>: On July 14, 1949, I wrote
                    letters to Secretary of Defense Johnson and to the Atomic Energy Commission
                    (copy <pb facs="0574" n="544" xml:id="pg544"/> enclosed, classified Top
                        Secret),<anchor n="2" xml:id="d199-f2"/> suggesting that production
                    requirements in the field of atomic energy be increased.</p>
                <p>Of course, I now adhere even more firmly to the views expressed in the letter of
                    July 14, and am passing it on in the hope that you may find it helpful in
                    considering whether or not a supplemental budget request should be forwarded to
                    the present Congress. I particularly call your attention to the fact that since
                    1945 only about one-thirtieth (1/30) of our expenditures directly for national
                    defense have been devoted to development of atomic energy.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brien McMahon</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d199-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d199-f1-content">
                <p>Senator McMahon addressed a separate letter to President Truman on September 28
                    dealing with the international control of atomic energy in light of the atomic
                    explosion in the Soviet Union; for text, see <ref target="#pg179">p.
                    179</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d199-f2" n="2" xml:id="d199-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg482">p. 482</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
